them with the pipeline scripts.


3. Evidence quotes are excluded from the CC-BY-4.0 grant
--------------------------------------------------------

Every benchmark item carries one or more `evidence` entries containing a
**verbatim quotation from a source publication**. Those quotations are not ours
and are **not** covered by section 1. They remain under the terms of their
source, and are included here as short attributed excerpts under fair use and
the corresponding quotation exceptions in other jurisdictions.

The extent of the quotation is small and measurable:

  * 301 quotes across 242 items -- about 1.2 per item;
  * median 163 characters, mean 170, maximum 480;
  * drawn from 175 distinct publications, i.e. under two quotes per paper;
  * each is a single passage, used to substantiate one benchmark answer.

Each quote is attributable. Its `evidence` entry carries `source_doi` and
`source_license`, and `analysis/outputs/source_papers.csv` maps every
`paper_id` to its title, DOI, year, and licence. Of the 175 source
publications, 160 resolve to a DOI; the remaining 15 are grey literature
(theses, preprints, older workshop papers) recorded by title.

Source licences, as recorded by Crossref (per publication / per quote):

    not-stated in Crossref            75  (43%) / 133 quotes
    publisher-specific                51  (29%) /  90
    publisher copyright (ACM)         18  (10%) /  35
    publisher TDM (Elsevier)          12   (7%) /  17
    CC-BY                              9   (5%) /  12
    arXiv, per-paper terms             9   (5%) /  13
    CC-BY-NC-ND                        1   (1%) /   1

Only 9 of 175 sources (5%) carry a licence that grants redistribution. This is
why the benchmark as a whole is not offered under CC-BY-4.0: doing so would
purport to license text the project does not own.

Two caveats, stated so the table is not read as stronger than it is.
"Not-stated" means Crossref holds no licence record, not that no licence
exists; several of those are likely preprints under open terms, so the table
overstates restrictiveness at the margin. And Crossref licence URLs describe
the publisher's posted terms, which may differ from the terms attached to a
specific version of record.

**If you redistribute or build on the benchmark**, the quotations travel with
it under this same limitation. If your use requires more than short quotation
-- for example redistributing the quoted passages as a text corpus in their own
right -- clear it against each source licence using `source_papers.csv`. If you
need the benchmark without third-party text, drop the `evidence` field; the
questions, reference answers, and all labels remain fully usable under
section 1.


4. Third-party model outputs
----------------------------

The released evaluation data includes text generated by third-party models:
answers from the evaluated open-weight and API models, and scores and
rationales from the two judge models. These are included as the experimental
record. Use of that text is additionally subject to the terms of the provider
that generated it. The evaluated open-weight models carry their own licences
(Llama Community Licence, Gemma Terms of Use, Apache-2.0 for Qwen); the exact
identifiers are listed in `NetBench-LLM/utils/download_models.py`, and the
licence that applies is the one attached to that model's own repository.


